# T22654 — modbus — write — tb470 IE520 stack — 2026-09-29 — PARTIAL

Verdict: PARTIAL. Steps 1, 2, 4, 5 PASS (alarm-configuration write, port-configured-state write,
and the port write repeated over a global IPv6 address and over link-local, each confirmed by
read-back, CLI and the DUT log). Step 3 (PoE configuration write, register 0x5002) is
UNSUPPORTED BY PLATFORM: no PoE on the AT-IE520-28GSX (`show power-inline` = `% Invalid
input`); the server answers the write with a Modbus exception and logs "Request failed".
Run labelled: clean. One finding worth Terrence's eye (not a failure): the alarm-config word
is MSB-first — LED = 0x8000, not 0x0001 — and a write of an unmapped bit (0x0001) is
acknowledged and logged as performed with no effect (see step 1).

Operator: bench-runner subagent (device-testing-61 = sentinel; Terrence away). Queue row 8,
case 2 of 6. Hand-driven: ckcon.py on /dev/u5 (stack master, member 3) + mb.py (pymodbus
3.8.6) from tb470 eth1. Full stdout: 22654.out (first pass, steps 1–5 + teardown),
22654-alarm-discovery.out (the step-1 investigation, second teardown); client logs mb-22654.log.
DUT clock runs ~12 h 50 min behind tb470 (DUT "00:51" = tb470 13:51); `show log` lines below
carry DUT time.

## Case (ck.db AWPTCM-T22654)
Objective: write to registers for Alarm Configuration, Port Configured State, Port PoE
Configuration. Steps: 1 write 0x3001 #1 Alarm Configuration (bit 0 = LED, bit n = Relay n),
confirm by read and CLI; 2 write 0x5001 #1 Port Configured State (4 nibbles: enabled, duplex,
polarity, speed), confirm write, log, read, CLI; 3 write 0x5002 #1 Port PoE Configuration;
4 repeat one write with a global IPv6 address; 5 repeat one write with a link-local IPv6 address.

## Setup (13:50:09, console)
`scada modbus tcp server` / `scada modbus tcp server access read-write` → `show scada modbus
tcp server`: "MODBUS is enabled / Write access: enabled / Port 502"; running-config gained
`scada modbus tcp server access read-write` + `scada modbus tcp server`; port1.0.1 running-config
= `switchport` / `switchport mode access` (no shutdown line); no `alarm` lines. Pre-test config
= pre-test-configs/u5.show_running-config.txt (13:42 probe capture).
Discovery (13:50:21, unit id 1): 0x3000 = 1 (alarm #1 type = External PSU, member 1's first
alarm, matching `show alarm facility settings` "Stack member 1: External PSU 1"); 0x3001 = 0x0000
(no outputs configured); 0x5001 = 0xf000 (port1.0.1 admin up, all auto).

## STEP 1 — write 0x3001 alarm #1 configuration (unit id 1 = member 1)
First pass (13:50:22, 22654.out), the case's literal "bit 0 = LED" as 0x0001:
  TX 00 01 00 00 00 06 01 06 30 01 00 01   WRITE 0x3001 = 0x0001 -> ok (echo addr=0x3001 value=0x0001)
  TX 00 01 00 00 00 06 01 03 30 01 00 01   READ  0x3001 -> 0x0000   (unchanged)
  CLI 13:50:28 `show alarm facility settings | include PSU`: External PSU 1 "-" on all members;
  `show running-config | include alarm`: empty.
  DUT log: "modbusd[531]: MODBUS: Performed write request from 10.38.215.1:43187: Write Single
  Register(FC=0x06) to member 1 starting at Stack member alarm info 1 Config (Members 1 to 4)
  (REG=0x3001) for 1 word with data 0x0001"  — accepted, logged, no effect, no error.
Cross-check against the CLI (13:55:38, 22654-alarm-discovery.out): `alarm facility power-supply
1 led` → running-config `alarm facility power-supply 1 input-member 1|3|4 led`, `show alarm
facility settings` External PSU 1 = "L" on members 1, 3, 4; then
  READ 0x3000 x6 unit 1 -> [0x0001, 0x8000, 0, 0, 0, 0x0000]      (config word = 0x8000)
  READ 0x3000 x6 unit 3 -> [0x0001, 0x8000, 0, 0, 0, 0xffff]      (status TRUE: member 3 "Power Input 1: No")
  READ 0x3000 x6 unit 0 -> same as unit 3 (unit 0 = the master's block)
  READ 0x3006 x6 unit 1 -> [0x0001, 0x0000, 0, 0, 0, 0xffff]      (alarm #2 External PSU 2, member 1 "Power Input 2: No")
  → the LED bit is the MSB (0x8000; relay n = 0x8000 >> n), exactly the ART decoder's
    `bitmap << bit & 0x8000`; the case text's "bit 0" is bit 0 counted from the top.
  → the mapping-5 alarm entry is SIX words [type, config, ?, ?, ?, status], not the three
    (type, config, status) the ART map assumes: status of alarm #1 is at 0x3005, alarm #2 starts
    at 0x3006. The full 93-word dump (unit 1, 31 alarms) is in 22654-alarm-discovery.out.
Modbus write with the right bit (13:55:58 onward, read-write on):
  TX 00 01 00 00 00 06 01 06 30 01 00 00   WRITE 0x3001 = 0x0000 -> ok; READ -> [1, 0x0000, …];
      CLI: member 1 External PSU 1 back to "-", running-config lost ONLY
      `alarm facility power-supply 1 input-member 1 led` (members 3, 4 kept theirs)  → a write CLEARS
  TX 00 01 00 00 00 06 01 06 30 01 00 01   WRITE 0x3001 = 0x0001 -> ok; READ -> 0x0000 (no effect, again)
  TX 00 01 00 00 00 06 01 06 30 0d 00 01   WRITE 0x300d = 0x0001 (alarm #3 = Link down port1.0.1) -> ok; READ -> 0x0000
  13:57:05  TX 00 01 00 00 00 06 01 06 30 01 80 00   WRITE 0x3001 = 0x8000 -> ok (echo addr=0x3001 value=0x8000)
            READ 0x3000 x6 unit 1 -> [0x0001, 0x8000, 0x0000, 0x0000, 0x0000, 0x0000]
            CLI 13:57:12: External PSU 1 "L" on member 1; running-config gained
            `alarm facility power-supply 1 input-member 1 led`
            DUT log: "MODBUS: Performed write request from 10.38.215.1:15053: Write Single Register
            (FC=0x06) to member 1 starting at Stack member alarm info 1 Config (Members 1 to 4)
            (REG=0x3001) for 1 word with data 0x8000"
  13:57:16  WRITE 0x300d = 0x8000 (alarm #3, Link down port1.0.1) -> ok; READ 0x300c x6 ->
            [0x0004, 0x8000, 0, 0, 0, 0xffff]; CLI: "Link down port1.0.1 L"; running-config gained
            `alarm facility link-down port1.0.1 led`; log "… alarm info 3 Config … (REG=0x300d) … data 0x8000"
  13:57:27  WRITE 0x3001 = 0x0000 on units 1, 3, 4 and 0x300d = 0x0000 on unit 1 -> all ok;
            read-backs config 0x0000 everywhere; `show running-config | include alarm` EMPTY;
            settings "-" for External PSU 1 on all members and for Link down port1.0.1.
Verdict step 1: PASS — the write is performed, read back (0x8000), and the CLI shows it
(`show alarm facility settings` "L", running-config line); the Modbus write also removes a
CLI-made setting. Observation for Terrence/Test-cases: bit numbering is MSB-first, and an
unmapped bit value (0x0001) is silently accepted (FC06 echo, "Performed write request" in the
log) with no effect and no exception — a client cannot tell from the response alone.

